05:01:24.808 --> 05:01:32.328
than what the capacity permits, it means something
has to go wrong. When an edge becomes overcapacity

05:01:32.328 --> 05:01:37.718
in some manner, in means that we've pushed
the system past its limit. In the context

05:01:37.718 --> 05:01:42.898
of edges representing pipes with water it
means your pipe broke or it leaked. If your

05:01:42.898 --> 05:01:48.820
edges a wire with electric current, it means
your wire literally fried or melted exploded

05:01:48.820 --> 05:01:53.298
or something bad happened to it because there
was too much electric current. This is not

05:01:53.298 --> 05:01:58.790
good. So this is why we don't allow more flow
than capacity. each edge in the flow graph

05:01:58.790 --> 05:02:06.370
has a certain flow and capacity specified
by the two values separated by a slash adjacent

05:02:06.370 --> 05:02:11.899
to each edge. Originally, the flow through
each edge is zero and the capacity is a non

05:02:11.899 --> 05:02:18.480
negative value to find the maximum flow and
also the min cut as a byproduct. The Ford

05:02:18.480 --> 05:02:25.988
Fulkerson method repeatedly finds augmenting
paths through the residual graph and augments

05:02:25.988 --> 05:02:32.020
the flow until no more augmenting paths can
be found. So you're probably asking yourself

05:02:32.020 --> 05:02:37.200
at this moment, what is an augmenting path?
What the heck is a residual graph? And what

05:02:37.200 --> 05:02:42.790
do you mean by augment the flow? All right,
let me explain. We'll do them one by one.

05:02:42.790 --> 05:02:48.398
an augmenting path is a path of edges in the
residual graph with capacity greater than

05:02:48.398 --> 05:02:55.718
zero from the source s to the sink t in orange.
Here I have highlighted a possible augmenting

05:02:55.718 --> 05:03:01.988
path. The key thing to remember about an augmenting
path is that it can only flow through edges

05:03:01.988 --> 05:03:07.738
which aren't fully saturated yet. In fact,
you know you've achieved the maximum flow

05:03:07.738 --> 05:03:13.738
when there are no more augmenting paths left
to be found. How to actually find an augmenting

05:03:13.738 --> 05:03:19.708
path is a detail left unspecified by the Ford
Fulkerson method for flexibility. For now

05:03:19.708 --> 05:03:25.238
let's assume that we're using a depth first
search. Something else to know is that every

05:03:25.238 --> 05:03:31.828
augmenting path will have what I call a bottleneck
value, which is the smallest edge along the

05:03:31.828 --> 05:03:32.828
path,

05:03:32.828 --> 05:03:36.898
you can find the value of the bottleneck by
taking the difference between the capacity

05:03:36.898 --> 05:03:42.860
and the current flow of an edge. For this
